{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 49, :comments ["<b>And his appointees, whom YHWH commanded Moses</b> – The Masorah has ״אֲשֶׁר״ written but read as ״כַּאֲשֶׁר״, and those counted were under the command from age thirty up to age fifty — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 4:49). If so, <b>and his appointees</b> would be an epithet for Moses. But Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that <b>each man</b> over Gershon, Kehat, and Merari means that Moses appointed each one of the three of them over his service and over his burden, as mentioned above, and this is the sense of <b>and his appointees</b> — of each and every man mentioned. And it is possible that <b>whom [he] commanded</b> may be understood in its plain sense: he says that he appointed these three brothers, each one over his service and over his burden, as is spelled out above, and the appointees of each of them whom YHWH commanded Moses to count by their polls — he appointed over his service and over his burden. He mentioned that he appointed them by their families, and he appointed the individuals by their polls, each one over his service and over his burden. And so too the law is that no Levite is permitted to perform his fellow's labor or even to assist in it, as they said (Sifrei Korach 9): And Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananyah once sought to assist Rabbi Yochanan ben Gudgeda in the setting of doors. He said to him: Go back, for you are already liable to death, for I am of the gatekeepers and you are of the singers."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "Ramban refers to Ibn Ezra here as ר״א (Rabbi Abraham); the epithet has been resolved as such based on the content matching Ibn Ezra's interpretation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:46.471156758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}